in 2-4 sequential stages where each stage's :writes feed the next stage's :reads. The proven shape uses :llm for content transformation and :code for deterministic formatting/aggregation at the tail. Always declare :output-schemas on intermediates so shape mismatches identify the offending stage, not the downstream consumer. Collapse very short successive stages into a single :llm to save tokens. Avoid when work is per-chunk independent (use chunked-extraction) or stages can run in parallel (use :parallel).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - ordered chain of typically 2-4 stages where each stage transforms the previous stage's output into a richer form\n  - natural fit for raw-input → normalized → enriched → emit-ready flows\n  - each stage's :reads consume the previous stage's :writes — explicit data dependency tracking\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** explicit per-stage :reads/:writes dependency chain makes ETL debuggable — failures isolate to the stage that produced the broken intermediate (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: input requires multiple distinct transformation passes AND each pass produces a named intermediate (normalized, enriched, formatted)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:raw-input] :writes [:normalized]}] [:llm {:reads [:normalized :reference-data] :writes [:enriched]}] [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:emit-ready] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:emit-ready (clojure.string/join \",\" enriched)})}] [:final {:keys [:emit-ready]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :code (NOT :llm) for the final emit-format stage when the format is deterministic — saves tokens and avoids drift in field names (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final stage is mechanical formatting (CSV row assembly, JSON envelope wrapping, fixed-width record padding)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:row] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:row (clojure.string/join \",\" (map :v enriched))})}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** ETL chains break when an intermediate stage silently emits a different shape than the next stage expects — typed :output-schemas catch the shape mismatch at the wrong stage (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: intermediate stages don't declare :output-schemas and downstream stages assume specific keys\n    - Recommended fix: always declare :output-schemas on each intermediate stage AND validate at the next stage's :reads — push the shape contract upstream so the failing stage is identified, not the consumer\n\n  - **Failure mode:** 5+ stage chains burn tokens that a single multi-instruction :llm could cover when stages are short (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: individual stages are 1-2 sentence transformations AND there's no need to inspect intermediates\n    - Recommended fix: collapse 3+ short successive :llm stages into a single :llm with a multi-step instruction and structured :output-schemas for each intermediate\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - customer record normalization: parse → dedupe-fields → enrich-from-CRM → emit-warehouse-row\n  - log line parsing: tokenize → classify-event-type → annotate-with-context → emit-structured-event\n  - translation pipeline: clean-input → translate → polish-style → emit-final-text\n\n#### Alternative #1 (confidence: 0.95)\nWhy this fits: Matches the structural shape of the intent (sequential multi-pass analysis and generation) and the ETL pattern of using :llm for content transformation (analysis/rewrite) and potentially :code for formatting. However, as a fingerprint, it lacks the explicit domain-child link found in the tree-class.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nETL pipelines chain 2-4 sequential stages where each stage's :writes feed the next stage's :reads. The proven shape uses :llm for content transformation and :code for deterministic formatting/aggregation at the tail. Always declare :output-schemas on intermediates so shape mismatches identify the offending stage, not the downstream consumer. Collapse very short successive stages into a single :llm to save tokens. Avoid when work is per-chunk independent (use chunked-extraction) or stages can run in parallel (use :parallel).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - ordered chain of typically 2-4 stages where each stage transforms the previous stage's output into a richer form\n  - natural fit for raw-input → normalized → enriched → emit-ready flows\n  - each stage's :reads consume the previous stage's :writes — explicit data dependency tracking\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** explicit per-stage :reads/:writes dependency chain makes ETL debuggable — failures isolate to the stage that produced the broken intermediate (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: input requires multiple distinct transformation passes AND each pass produces a named intermediate (normalized, enriched, formatted)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:raw-input] :writes [:normalized]}] [:llm {:reads [:normalized :reference-data] :writes [:enriched]}] [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:emit-ready] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:emit-ready (clojure.string/join \",\" enriched)})}] [:final {:keys [:emit-ready]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :code (NOT :llm) for the final emit-format stage when the format is deterministic — saves tokens and avoids drift in field names (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final stage is mechanical formatting (CSV row assembly, JSON envelope wrapping, fixed-width record padding)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:row] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:row (clojure.string/join \",\" (map :v enriched))})}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** ETL chains break when an intermediate stage silently emits a different shape than the next stage expects — typed :output-schemas catch the shape mismatch at the wrong stage (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: intermediate stages don't declare :output-schemas and downstream stages assume specific keys\n    - Recommended fix: always declare :output-schemas on each intermediate stage AND validate at the next stage's :reads — push the shape contract upstream so the failing stage is identified, not the consumer\n\n  - **Failure mode:** 5+ stage chains burn tokens that a single multi-instruction :llm could cover when stages are short (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: individual stages are 1-2 sentence transformations AND there's no need to inspect intermediates\n    - Recommended fix: collapse 3+ short successive :llm stages into a single :llm with a multi-step instruction and structured :output-schemas for each intermediate\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - customer record normalization: parse → dedupe-fields → enrich-from-CRM → emit-warehouse-row\n  - log line parsing: tokenize → classify-event-type → annotate-with-context → emit-structured-event\n  - translation pipeline: clean-input → translate → polish-style → emit-final-text\n\nAssigned to domain child 6db2a133-024a-3a29-afce-0b2f384c80b7 (label: sql-query-optimization) under the top match — this campaign's outcome is that child's first evidence.\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 3 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Investigation explains WHY a system (confidence: 0.95)\n   Why this fits: This task is a classic investigation: it starts with a symptom (a slow SQL query) and requires a root-cause diagnosis followed by a fix. The candidate's 'hypothesis-then-evidence' pattern (diagnose bottleneck, then propose fix) perfectly matches the multi-step structure requested (query-plan-analysis, then rewrite/index/risks). Its strength in 'enumerating candidate hypotheses upfront' aligns with identifying the most likely bottleneck (sequential scan, sort spill) before proposing the fix.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Investigation explains WHY a system is misbehaving. Best implemented as an explicit hypothesis-then-evidence shape: enumerate candidate causes from the symptom UPFRONT, then collect evidence to rule out alternatives, then converge on the root cause + recommended fix. ALWAYS pass ambient context (logs / code / tests / stack traces) in :reads — context-free investigation produces vacuous output. For large evidence sets (multi-file logs, large codebases), compose into chunked-extraction shells so per-chunk evidence collection parallelizes. Composes into sequential-pipeline (canonical hypothesis chain), fallback-recovery (try-one-path-then-another when initial hypothesis fails), and chunked-extraction (investigating large evidence sets). Distinct from Research (which gathers external material to seed new work, not explain existing breakage) and Analysis (which reasons over already-extracted items, not raw symptoms). Avoid when the task is exploration without a specific failure to explain.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given symptoms + ambient context (logs, code, test artifacts, stack traces), produce a structured hypothesis with supporting evidence and a recommended fix or next step\n  - natural fit when the goal is to explain WHY something is broken — distinct from Research (gather new material) and Analysis (reason over already-extracted items)\n  - outputs are typically a single root-cause statement + supporting evidence references + a recommended-fix or next-step\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** enumerate candidate hypotheses UPFRONT from the symptom before collecting evidence — separates 'what could be wrong' from 'what is wrong' so evidence rules out rather than confirms the first guess (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the failure mode space is bounded AND the model can enumerate likely causes from the symptom alone\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:symptom :context] :writes [:candidate-hypotheses]}] [:llm {:reads [:candidate-hypotheses :context] :writes [:ruled-out :remaining]}] [:llm {:reads [:remaining :context] :writes [:root-cause :recommended-fix]}] [:final {:keys [:root-cause :recommended-fix]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for investigations over large evidence sets (multi-file logs, large codebases), chunk the evidence and run per-chunk hypothesis-evidence collection via :map-each so per-chunk reasoning parallelizes and the aggregator sees per-chunk findings tagged with their source (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: evidence is too large to fit in a single :llm context AND per-chunk findings can be aggregated without losing per-chunk attribution\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:map-each {:items :evidence-chunks :as :chunk :max-concurrency 3 :writes [:per-chunk-findings]} [:llm {:reads [:chunk :symptom] :writes [:per-chunk-findings] :output-schemas {:per-chunk-findings [:vector [:map [:hypothesis :string] [:supporting-evidence :string] [:chunk-id :string]]]}}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** single-pass investigation on a complex symptom converges on the first plausible hypothesis without ruling out alternatives — produces overconfident root-cause claims that fail when the true cause was the second hypothesis (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the symptom has multiple plausible causes AND the cost of acting on the wrong root cause is high\n    - Recommended fix: explicit multi-hypothesis enumeration upfront; an intermediate :llm stage must rule out at least ONE candidate hypothesis with evidence before convergence to a root cause is allowed\n\n  - **Failure mode:** investigation without ambient context (just the symptom + nothing else) produces vacuous 'could be X / could be Y' output the consumer can't act on — the model has no evidence to rule anything in or out (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: context (logs / code / tests / stack traces) is available in the system but not passed in :reads\n    - Recommended fix: always pass ambient context explicitly in :reads; if the context is too large for a single :llm node, compose into chunked-extraction via :map-each so per-chunk evidence reaches the hypothesis-evidence stage\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - investigate why an integration test is flaky: enumerate timing / shared-state / environment hypotheses; rule out via test logs + run history; recommend the deterministic fix\n  - generate property-based tests that exercise edge cases — property test generation IS investigation, finding inputs that break invariants\n  - debug a memory leak in a long-running service: enumerate allocator / reference-cycle / cache-bloat hypotheses; rule out via heap snapshots + allocation profiles; recommend the targeted fix\n  - review a post-incident report for missing root-cause analysis: enumerate plausible causes the report didn't address; surface the gap with evidence from the timeline\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                                     :confidence 0.7\n                                     :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :weaknesses [{:trait \"<what fails AND why>\"\n                                      :avoid-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                      :recommended-alternative \"<concrete fix>\"\n                                      :confidence 0.7\n                                      :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :representative-uses [\"<concrete task this child shipped on>\"]\n                        :avoid-when [\"<concrete anti-context>\"]\n                        :summary \"<2-3 sentences naming the domain + load-bearing trait + when to prefer this over the parent>\"\n                        :version 1\n                        :consolidated-from-event-count 0}\n                       :parent 760be698-0bb8-3a5a-a2bd-1d45445a5861)\n\n   - MINT-ADJACENT — if your task is related-but-DISTINCT (not a child of this behavior), mint an adjacent behavior the same way with `:parent nil` (or under whichever listed reference is the true nearest parent).\n   :strengths/:weaknesses are VECTORS OF MAPS (principle-shaped: :trait + context-guard + concrete recommended action + :confidence + :evidence-count) — a vector of bare strings fails schema validation; the rejection is returned in iteration history for correction.\n\n2. Behavioral suggestion (confidence: 0.90)\n   Why this fits: This candidate addresses the core goal of making code (SQL) faster while preserving output (invariance). Its 'measure-then-change-then-re-measure' loop matches the requirement to provide an expected improvement and a verification plan. It correctly notes that this behavior is often a composition of Investigation (diagnosis) and Code-building (fixing), which mirrors the task's flow.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Performance-optimization makes existing code faster WITHOUT changing what it produces — a measure-then-change-then-re-measure loop (really Investigation to find the hot path, then Code-building to optimize) under an output-invariance constraint. The load-bearing traits are profiling to locate the real bottleneck BEFORE editing (never guess from reading the code) and pinning output-invariance with a before/after comparison (a speedup that changes results is a regression). Prefer this over the broad Transformation parent whenever the task pairs a speed target with 'without changing the output': Transformation's one-shot 'reshape in :code' shape ships an unmeasured, possibly output-altering change. Avoid when the task adds new behavior (code-building) or has no correctness constraint (the Transformation parent).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given code that is too slow + a performance target + an output-invariance constraint, locate the hot path, change it for speed, and prove the output is unchanged\n  - natural fit when the task is 'X is slow, make it faster WITHOUT changing what it produces' — a measure-then-change-then-re-measure loop, not a one-shot reshape\n  - outputs are typically a code edit to the hot path + a before/after measurement + evidence the observable output is byte-for-byte unchanged\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** measure to find the hot path BEFORE editing — optimize what the profile proves is slow, not what looks slow; premature edits to cold code add risk for no gain (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the slow region is not obvious AND a profiling/timing signal is available\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:code {:reads [:input] :writes [:profile] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:profile :hot-path})}] [:llm {:reads [:profile :src] :writes [:optimized-src]}] [:code {:reads [:optimized-src] :writes [:after-timing]}] [:final {:keys [:optimized-src :after-timing]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** pin output-invariance with a check that compares optimized output to the original on representative inputs — a speedup that changes results is a regression, not an optimization (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the task explicitly requires 'without changing the output' (or correctness matters more than speed)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:writes [:optimized-src]}] [:code {:reads [:optimized-src :baseline-output] :writes [:invariance-check] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:invariance-check :outputs-equal})}] [:final {:keys [:optimized-src :invariance-check]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** optimizing by rewriting the logic (the Transformation parent's 'reshape it in :code' instinct) without a baseline measurement OR an invariance check ships a change that may be no faster and may silently alter output (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the task pairs a speed target with an output-invariance constraint\n    - Recommended fix: measure first (profile/timing), change only the proven hot path, then compare optimized output to the baseline before committing\n\n  - **Failure mode:** guessing the bottleneck from reading the code instead of profiling wastes the edit on the wrong region and often misses the real cost (I/O, N+1, allocation) entirely (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: a profiler or representative-input timing harness is available\n    - Recommended fix: always derive the hot path from a measurement stage; let the profile, not intuition, choose where to edit\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - the report-rendering endpoint is slow on large inputs; profile it and optimize the hot path so a 10k-row report renders under 500ms without changing the output\n  - a nightly aggregation job exceeds its window; find and fix the dominant cost while keeping the produced totals identical\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<c